  "query_preview": "..Arizona remains under a very dynamic weather pattern\ntoday with upper level low pressure centered over western Arizona\nand moisture from Hurricane Polo streaming northward. Continued\nshower/thunderstorm development is expected through tonight and\ninto early Wednesday morning before coming to an end. A Flood\nWatch remains in effect for all of northern Arizona through\nthis evening, and has been extended slightly across northeastern\nArizona until early Wednesday morning.\n\nThus far, widespread rainfall has been observed across northern\nArizona with the heaviest amounts favoring areas along and south\nof the Mogollon Rim and White Mountains. Many of these locations\nhave seen amounts of 1.00 - 2.50 inches. Further north, rainfall\namounts have been in the 0.50 - 1.00 inch range. All of this\nantecedent rainfall is saturating the ground across northern \nArizona and will lead to a faster flood response with additional \nrain through the rest of today and tonight. The most widespread \nrain today has been across eastern Arizona, and this area will \ncontinue to be favored with potentially an additional 1-2 inches \nof rain. Further west, enough clearing in the dry slot of the low \nhas allowed scattered convection to develop. The enhanced moisture\nwill interact with limited instability to support this \ndevelopment through this evening. Though heavy rainfall and flash \nflooding are the primary threats, strong 0-6km wind shear of \n40-60+ kts will result in a secondary threat for isolated severe \nstorms (large hail and damaging wind). \n\nLater tonight as the upper level low progresses across northern \nArizona, moisture wrapping around the northern periphery will \nbring another round of rain with a few embedded thunderstorms. The\nlatest hi-res model guidance favors this activity to remain \nprimarily along and north of the Mogollon Rim and this is why the \nFlood Watch was extended there overnight. \n\nShowers will continue across eastern portions of the state on\nWednesday as the low moves into New Mexico. The rest of the area \nwill see improving conditions through the day. Temperatures will \nremain quite cool through Wednesday given the rainfall and trough \npassage. Thursday morning will be quite cool in the wake of the \ntrough with clearing skies and some high elevations cold spots may\ndrop close to freezing.\n\nThe remainder of the week features high pressure building across\nthe southwest, resulting in a drier period with near average\ntemperatures. The dry weather looks to persist into next week.",
